Part 3: Understanding Domain Model

Core Domain Entities

The central domain entity is the "Task" class in "models.py".

A Task represents a piece of work that the user wants to track. It contains information such as a title, description, priority, status, creation time, update time, due date, completion time, and tags.

The application also defines two important enums:

- "TaskStatus" — represents the current state of a task.
- "TaskPriority" — represents the importance of a task.

Domain Relationship Diagram

                    ┌─────────────────┐
                    │      Task       │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             │
             ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
             │               │                │
             ↓               ↓                ↓
       TaskStatus       TaskPriority        Tags
       TODO             LOW                 labels
       IN_PROGRESS      MEDIUM
       REVIEW            HIGH
       DONE              URGENT

A Task has one status and one priority at a time. A Task can also have multiple tags.

Business Logic

The "Task" class contains important business behaviour.

The "update()" method allows task attributes to be changed and updates the "updated_at" timestamp.

The "mark_as_done()" method changes the task status to "DONE", records the completion time in "completed_at", and updates "updated_at".

The "is_overdue()" method determines whether a task is overdue. A task is considered overdue when it has a due date that is earlier than the current time and its status is not "DONE".

Initial Questions

Before examining the model more deeply, I had questions about:

1. Whether changing priority automatically changes task status.
2. Whether a higher priority automatically causes a task to be processed first.
3. What makes a task overdue.
4. Which component is responsible for changing task state.
5. How status and priority are stored when tasks are saved.

Revised Understanding

After examining the code, I learned that priority and status are separate concepts. Changing a task's priority does not automatically change its status.

I also learned that priority does not automatically reorder tasks. It is used for assigning, filtering, and counting tasks.

Task completion is handled by the "mark_as_done()" method, which changes the status and records completion information.

Domain Glossary

Term| Meaning
Task| A piece of work tracked by the application
TaskStatus| The current state of a task
TODO| A task that has not started
IN_PROGRESS| A task currently being worked on
REVIEW| A task waiting for or undergoing review
DONE| A completed task
TaskPriority| The importance level assigned to a task
LOW| Lowest priority level
MEDIUM| Default priority level
HIGH| High priority level
URGENT| Highest priority level
Due date| The date by which a task should be completed
Tag| A label associated with a task
completed_at| The date and time when a task was completed
updated_at| The date and time when a task was last updated
Overdue| A task whose due date has passed and which is not DONE